(Primary Care Intervention and Quality of Life Trajectories in Post-ICU Trauma: One-Year) Tj
T*
(Outcomes) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(We randomized 319 ICU survivors with moderate-to-severe PTSD symptoms to either a brief) Tj
T*
(primary-care-delivered psychological intervention \(n = 159\) or enhanced usual care \(n =) Tj
T*
(160\). The intervention consisted of 4-6 sessions with general practitioners trained in) Tj
T*
(structured exposure techniques. At 12-month follow-up, the intervention group showed) Tj
T*
(greater improvement in health-related quality of life scores \(mean improvement 0.071) Tj
T*
(points on 0-1 quality-of-life scale, 95% CI 0.010-0.132\) compared to controls. This) Tj
T*
(difference emerged gradually, with no significant between-group difference at 6 months but) Tj
T*
(substantial divergence by 12 months. PTSD symptom severity decreased more substantially in) Tj
T*
(the intervention group \(mean reduction 12.3 points on symptom scale vs 6.8 points, p =) Tj
T*
(0.004\). Domain-specific analyses showed significant improvements in anxiety/depression and) Tj
T*
(physical functioning scores in the intervention group. Approximately 58% of the) Tj
T*
(intervention group and 41% of controls reported subjectively meaningful improvements in) Tj
T*
(overall quality of life. The intervention was well-tolerated with high completion rates) Tj
T*
(\(78% completed >=3 of 6 planned sessions\). These findings suggest that structured primary) Tj
T*
(care interventions may improve long-term quality of life in ICU survivors with PTSD.) Tj
